# Charging Metal‐Organic Framework Membranes by Incorporating Crown Ethers to Capture Cations for Ion Sieving

## abstract
Protein channels on the biofilm conditionally manipulate ion transport via regulating the distribution of charge residues, making analogous processes on artificial membranes a hot spot and challenge. Here, we employ metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) membrane with charge-adjustable subnano-channel to selectively govern ion transport. Various valent ions are binded with crown ethers embedded in the MOF cavity, which act as charged guest to regulate the channels charge state from the negativity to positivity. Compared with the negatively charged channel, the positive counterpart obviously enhances Li+ /Mg2+ selectivity, which benefit from the reinforcement of the electrostatic repulsion between ions and the channel. Meanwhile, theoretical calculations reveal that Mg2+ transport through the more positively charged channel needed to overcome higher entrance energy barrier than that of Li+ . This work provides a subtle strategy for ion-selective transport upon regulating the charge state of insulating membrane, which paves the way for the application like seawater desalination and lithium extraction from salt lakes.

## Introduction
Efficient ion selectivity of protein channel is essential for biofilm system.[1] Studies have shown that the stimulusresponse compatibility of ion/molecular transport is attributed to the transitions between nano-channel conformations and the regulated distribution of charged residues in the nano-channels (i.e., the transient receptor potential channels).[2–3] In a potential nanochannel, the local charge environment is a flexible regulating factor, and the effect of the electrostatic interactions between ions and channels are amplified by the nano-confinement.[4] Notably, the nanoscale protein channels plays a crucial role in ion selectivity, and the influence of short-range forces becomes significant in extremely confined spaces, such as van der Waals forces, coordination bonds and hydrogen bonds.[5–6] In addition, the effect of the electrostatic interactions is amplified due to the increased Bjerrum length and the decrease in the dielectric coefficient in nano-confined spaces.[7–8] Therefore, remodeled charge environment of nano-channel preferentially binds with targeted ion to enhance the selectivity. As a result, fabricating artificial channel with analogous functions are of growing concern. How to precisely control the charge environment in channel deepen our understanding of ion transport across biofilm. However, this ideal process requires mimicking charge regulation in biological ion channels to surmount the thermodynamical and engineering constraints.
Inspired by biological ion channels, artificial nanochannel with modulable surface charges have been fabricated to mimic the electrostatic interaction of natural nanochannels for controlling ion transport.[9] For example, with conductive two-dimensional (2D) graphene films, the surface charge can be easily controlled by applied-voltage modulation,[10] thus, altering the energy barrier for ion intercalation into the subnano-scale channel.[11] Apart from voltage modulation, specific ion species might be utilized to alter electrical double layer (EDL) for affecting ion transport activity.[12–13] It has been demonstrated that the redistribution of ions in the EDL in the nanochannels leads to an anomalous enhancement of ionic diffusion.[14] We envisage that ions could be introduced into nanoconfined channel to enable charge regulation. However, what currently lacking is the universal charge regulation in other non-conductive channels that mimic the ion-selectivity behavior of biological ones, as anchor charge in an applied voltage free manner is grand challenging (Figure 1a). Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) with suitable pore sizes and modifiable cavity may serve as biomimetic ion filters, and their nanoconfined channel could accommodate guest molecules via host–guest effect.[15–16] Crown ether is usually effective at anchoring cations due to its cyclic cavities and electron donating properties.[17–18] Therefore, we envision that crown ethers encapsulated in MOFs’ channel could serve as potential ion-anchoring sites to bind different cations, which enable to achieve charge regulation in artificial membranes.
Herein, we reported a series efficient ion screening MOFs (i.e., ZIF-7) with adjustable charged membranes based on crowning cations (Figure 1b). The negatively charged ZIF-7 with suitable cavity size allows the encapsulating of crown ether, which combines with the cation to become a charged guest. And these guests can regulate charge property of MOF channel flexibly even inverse the surface charge from the negative to positive by some trivalence cations (Figure 1c). Compared with the negatively charged channels, the positive counterparts provide stronger electrostatic repulsion on $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{g}^{2+}$ counterpart for $\mathrm{Li^{+}}$ and contribute a higher entry energy for $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{g}^{2+}$ . This rare charge inversion of MOF channel achieves the enhanced $\mathrm{Li^{+}}/\mathrm{Mg^{2+}}$ selectivity from ca. 25 to 125.
## Results and Discussion
The fact that tunable pore size can be reconciled into a suitable cavity ensure zeolitic imidazolate frameworks (ZIFs) showing promising potential on limiting guest molecules via variety methods.[16] We firstly mixed the Zinc ions $(Z\boldsymbol{\mathrm{n}}^{2+})$ and benzo-12-crown-4-ether (BCE) together as complex-precursors and then adopted one-pot strategy to synthesis BCE@ZIF-7 by stirring with benzimidazole, which makes the BCE successfully encapsulated into ZIF-7 channel in the fabrication (Figure 1b). BCE is an excellent ion-anchor mainly based on the following two consideration: 1) the size of BCE $(\approx8\mathrm{\AA})$ is smaller than the cavity size $(\approx8.7\mathrm{\AA})$ but larger than the window size of ZIF-7 ( ${\approx}4.9\mathrm{~\AA~}$ ) (Figure S1); 2) BCE can conformationally coordinate with different cations[17] to be “the charging guest” (BCE complex cations, called as BCEC). According to the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption/desorption isotherm diagram, the variation tendency of BCE@ZIF-7 is similar to ZIF-7 (Figure 2a). From the pore size distribution (Figure 2b), the pore window of BCE@ZIF-7 is $0.6\mathring\mathrm{\mathrm{A}}$ narrower than ZIF-7 at of ${\approx}4.3\mathrm{~\AA~}$ . Both descending surface areas and pore size distributions confirm the existence of guesting BCE in ZIF7. These results are consistent with Fourier transform infrared spectroscopy result (FTIR) (Figure 2c), in which
BCE@ZIF-7 shows the stretching of $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{C-O-C}}$ around 1075 and $1265\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ . The above results confirmed that BCE molecules were trapped inside the ZIF-7 cavities during the ZIF-7 crystallization process. BCEC accounted for the charge state of BCE@ZIF-7, which could be finely tuned by binding various valent cations. A regular order of MOF surface zeta potential (Figure 2d) suggests that the charge properties can be easily modulated via a simple pretreatment (see experimental details). In water, the surface charge of ZIF-7 is negative because of deprotonation of benzimidazole $\mathrm{(-18.5mV)}$ . With the valence of the crowned cations increase, the charge state of MOF will change from the negative to positive. The zeta potential of $\mathrm{Li}[\mathrm{BCE}]^{+}@\mathrm{ZIF}{-}7$ and $\mathrm{Mg}[\mathrm{BCE}]^{2}{}^{+}\textcircled{\omega}\mathrm{ZIF}{}{}\cdot{}7$ are $-12.1$ and $0.8\mathrm{mV}$ , respectively, and $\mathrm{Al}[\mathrm{BCE}]^{3+}@\mathrm{ZIF}-7$ exhibits most positive charge properties with surface potential of $16.2\mathrm{mV}$ . The X-ray diffraction (XRD) pattern (Figure 2e) indicates that the introduction of BCE during crystallization process would not affect the original structure.
We adopted a two-step method to prepare ZIF-7 composite membrane (see experimental details). Firstly, the as-synthesized BCE@ZIF-7 was immobilized on the nylon substrate. Secondly, fluid epoxy[18] was casted onto a pre-prepared substrate, then scraped with a blade by setting the height as $20\upmu\mathrm{m}$ . It should be noticed the epoxy could filling defects (Figure S2a). This composite system is denoted as ZIF-7 membrane or BCE $@$ ZIF-7 membrane, with which the MOF channels are the dominant pathways for ion permeation. In this case, the selectivity of the composite membrane is significantly independent on the properties of the non-selective polymer matrix. Figure S2b shows the surface morphology of ZIF-7 membrane under scanning electron microscopy (SEM). Since there is no visible defect in SEM image, this implies that the selection layer completely covers over the substrate and demonstrating the dense nature of the membrane. Cross-section SEM image (Figure S2b) displays MOF particles with size in tens of microns penetrating epoxy. The pure nylon substrate or Epoxy@Nylon membrane almost display non ion selectivity (Figure S3). XRD measurement on the membrane (Figure S4) also reveals the integrity of MOF maintained.
In order to ensure that ions could adequately entry the MOF channel, the effect of ion pretreatment on membrane structure was observed by monitoring open circuit voltage (OCV) before the ion selectivity test. The OCV gradually decreased and stabilized around $30\mathrm{min}$ (Figure. S6), reflecting the saturation of ion concentration in MOF, which was also supported by the particle energy spectra (Figure 2f).
Next, the ion transport properties of ZIF-7 and BCE@ZIF-7 membranes are investigated by measuring the current–voltage $(I{-}V)$ curve[19] of a certain salt solution separated by the membrane under external potential (Figure 3a). Here, we mainly adopted $0.1\mathbf{M}$ LiCl and $\mathbf{MgCl}_{2}$ to study the cationic transport (Figure S5). As shown in Figure 3b and 3c, $I{-}V$ curves for ZIF-7 and BCE@ZIF-7 exhibit centrosymmetric features to that of bulk solutions (Figure S7a). At the given potential, the current of the pure epoxy membrane in different salts is negligible indicating the good sealing property, thus the MOFs are the main channels for ion migration. The slope of $I{-}V$ curve corresponds to the ion conductance value. For membrane measurement, the higher the slope, the more the ion flux. The $\mathrm{Li^{+}/M g^{2+}}$ separation ratio of the ZIF-7 membrane is ca. 25 (Figure 3d), as its pore windows is smaller than the hydrated ion, which exhibit significant energy barrier. The dehydration energy of $\mathrm{Li^{+}}$ is much smaller than that of $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{g}^{2+}$ . Hence, the $\mathrm{Li^{+}}$ is easier to enter the MOF channel.[20] More importantly, the presence of BCEC in the channel mediates the transport of different cations. Specifically, by adjusting the negative charge state $(\mathrm{Li}[\mathrm{BCE}]^{+}@\mathrm{ZIF}-7)$ to positive charge state $\left(\mathrm{\bfAl}\right[\mathrm{\bfBCE}]^{3+}$ ${\ @{\cal Z}\mathrm{IF}}{-7}\rangle$ , the subtle changes of the LiCl current in different $\mathrm{\bfM}[\mathrm{BCE}]^{\mathrm{n}+}@\mathrm{ZIF}-7$ membranes was investigated, yet the $\mathbf{MgCl}_{2}$ current varied more significantly. Consequently, $\mathrm{Li^{+}}$ $/\mathbf{M}\mathbf{g}^{2+}$ separation ratio showing gradual rises, a high $\mathrm{Li^{+}}/$ $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{g}^{2+}$ selectivity of ca. 125 was achieved in the positively charged membrane $(\mathrm{AI}[\mathrm{BCE}]^{3+}@\mathrm{ZIF}-7)$ , while the negatively charged membrane $(\mathrm{Li}[\mathrm{BCE}]^{+}@\mathrm{ZIF}-7)$ showed a lower selectivity of ca. 73. In general, positively charged membranes are more likely to prevent the transport of divalent cations than those with monovalent ones, while negatively charged membranes are less resistant to inhibiting cation migration. We speculate that the selectivity of $\mathrm{Li^{+}/M g^{2+}}$ may be determined by the electrostatic repulsion in the positively charged membrane. On the basis of constraint, electrostatic repulsion is an effective way to distinguish different cations. In addition, the change of the LiCl current in different $\mathrm{\bfM}[\mathrm{BCE}]^{\mathrm{n}+}@\mathrm{ZIF}-7$ membranes were subtle, whereas $\mathbf{MgCl}_{2}$ current varied more significantly.
In order to further understand the ionic transmembrane mechanism, the ion transport experiment at different temperatures was carried out (Figure S8). The ionic conductance through the ZIF-7 membrane were shown in Figure S9 indicates that both LiCl and $\mathbf{MgCl}_{2}$ undergo Arrhenius-like transport, with activation energy of 18.1 and $32.3\mathrm{kJ}\mathrm{mol}^{-1}$ , respectively. These values are significantly improved in the $\mathrm{Al}[\mathrm{BCE}]^{3+}@\mathrm{ZIF}-7.$ , with respective activation energy of 40.3 and $89.6\mathrm{kJ}\mathrm{mol}^{-1}$ , indicating a reduced salt flux. We noticed that the activation energy deviation between LiCl and $\mathrm{{MgCl}}_{2}$ was closely related to the charge properties of the membrane. However, the positively charged membrane could enlarge this activation energy deviation, which is beneficial for the increased $\mathrm{Li^{+}/M g^{2+}}$ selectivity.
What’s more, since it was found that the applied voltage has an impact on the ion selectivity of 2D nanofluidic,[20] the effect of voltage bias on the performance of $\mathrm{\bfAl[BCE]^{3+}}$ $\textcircled{a}Z\mathrm{IF}{-}7$ was investigated (Figure S10). We measured the current-time $\left(I\ –t\right)$ of $0.1\mathbf{M}$ LiCl and $\mathbf{MgCl}_{2}$ at different voltages to demonstrate the selective ion conduction in the MOF-based nanofluidic membrane. Due to the presence of hydrogen precipitation reaction, the upper limit of applied voltage bias was set to be $1.0\mathrm{V}$ . Compared with LiCl, the low level of $\mathbf{MgCl}_{2}$ current and the voltage bias change reconfirmed the low $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{g}^{2+}$ conduction in the $\mathbf{Al}[\mathbf{BCE}]^{3+}$ $\textcircled{a}Z\mathrm{IF}{-}7$ . The selectivity of $\mathrm{Li^{+}}/\mathrm{Mg^{2+}}$ increased from ca. 28.7 at $0.05\mathrm{V}$ to ca. 124.1 at $1.0\mathrm{V}$ , while the growth rate slows down with the increase of voltage bias, due to the stronger driving force that still facilitating the migration of $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{g}^{2+}$ . The same trend can be seen in the $\mathrm{Li}[\mathrm{BCE}]^{+}@\mathrm{ZIF}{-}7$ and $\mathrm{Mg}[\mathrm{BCE}]^{2}{}^{+}(\stackrel{\rightharpoonup}{\omega}\mathrm{ZIF}-7$ , either. In addition, $\mathrm{\bfAl[BCE]^{3+}}$ $\textcircled{a}Z\mathrm{IF}{-}7$ has good cyclic properties and superior stability in various solutions (Figure $\mathrm{\bfS9c-d}_{,\mathrm{\bf\Phi}}^{\mathrm{\bf\Phi}}$ ). What’s more, after immersing in different solutions for one week (Figure S11), the ion selectivity still keeps the same order of magnitude as original ones. The integrity of MOF membranes is almost the same as the original ones, suggesting the stability of MOF is great.
To test $\mathrm{Li^{+}}$ sieving potential in the more complex occasion, we then investigated the perm-selectivity of ZIF7 and $\mathrm{Al}[\mathrm{BCE}]^{3+}@\mathrm{ZIF}{-7}$ in binary ion systems. As illustrated in Figure. S14, the permeation of $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{g}^{2+}$ in ZIF-7 membrane is higher than that of $\mathbf{Al}[\mathbf{BCE}]^{3+}@\mathbf{ZIF}.$ . Also, it shows a difference between the phenomenon in the single solution and the mixed solution. We speculate that it is due to the mutual interference between the $\mathrm{Li^{+}}$ and $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{g}^{2+}$ in the mixed solution. $\mathrm{Li^{+}}$ and $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{g}^{2+}$ will compete to enter the channels, and the accumulated $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{g}^{2+}$ would occupy the negative channels. The present of $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{g}^{2+}$ would reduce the effective size of the channel, further leading to the decreased permeation rate of Li ions, which is similar to the more ordered EDL in highly confined spaces.[21] However, the $\mathrm{Al}[\mathrm{BCE}]^{3+}@\mathrm{ZIF}{-7}$ with positive zeta potential has a great electrostatic repulsion with $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{g}^{2+}$ , leading the alleviated interference of $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{g}^{2+}$ . It is the reason why $\mathrm{Li^{+}}$ permeation rate in $\scriptstyle\mathrm{Al}[\mathrm{BCE}]^{3+}@Z\mathrm{I}$ is higher than that in ZIF-7. Therefore, the change in ion transport behavior exacerbates difference in $\mathrm{Li^{+}/M g^{2+}}$ selectivity, which deserves a more in-depth study. According to density functional theory (DFT) simulations, it should be noted ${\bf A}{\bf l}^{3+}$ prefer to enter the ZIF-7 channel and bind to BCE, and the energy of this process is largest (Table S1). This is consistent with what we found in ion exchange measurements. $\mathrm{\bfAl}[\mathrm{\bfBCE}]^{3+}$ membrane have been tested in Li-ion solution for $12\mathrm{h}$ . As shown in Table S2, this Li-ion solution contained only sub ppb $(<\mathrm{ug/L})$ levels of Al after $12\mathrm{h}$ test via the inductively coupled plasma spectrometry analysis (ICP). Thus, these results demonstrated that ${\bf A}{\bf l}^{3+}$ stably existed in the BCE@ZIF7 membrane, which are consistent with DFT calculation model (Figure S16).
To support our hypothesis, we further examined the potential ion transport mechanism occurring in the surfacecharged $\mathrm{\bfM}[\mathrm{BCE}]^{\mathrm{n}+}@\mathrm{ZIF}-7$ by DFT simulations[22] (Figure 4a) to mimic the process of ion passing the pore window of MOF channel, and the structure of ZIF-7 refers to the literature.[23] As shown in Figure 4b, we considered two mainly defined states during the ionic transport: (i) ion outside the MOF (State 1); (ii) ion entering the pore window of MOF (state 2). It should be noted that due to the large dissolution energy, the theoretical energy barrier of ions in the hole is much smaller than the one when entering. The energy difference between state 1 and 2 reflects the entry energy of ions, and the introduction of BCEC increases the entry energy of $\mathbf{Li}^{+}$ and $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{g}^{2+}$ compared with the absence of BCEC in the channel. It is attributed to the abundant electronegative terminal atoms in the original ZIF-7 channel attracting $\mathrm{Li^{+}}$ and $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{g}^{2+}$ and resulting in the negative zeta potential, leading to the lower entry energy. Compared with the high charge density and strong hydrated $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{g}^{2+}$ , ${\bf L i}^{+}$ only needs to overcome a less dehydration energy to pass through the pore window, which is one of the key sources of selectivity $(\mathrm{Li^{+}}$ : $235.4\mathrm{kJmol}^{-1}$ , $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{g}^{2+}$ : $351.2\mathrm{kJmol}^{-1}$ . The synergistic effect of electrostatic attraction and steric hindrance help $\mathrm{Li^{+}}$ establish its transport advantage over $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{g}^{2+}$ . Fortunately, the electrostatic repulsion of the positively charged Al$[\mathrm{BCE}]^{3+}@\mathrm{ZIF}-7$ channel on $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{g}^{2+}$ is stronger than that on ${\mathrm{Li}^{+}}$ , and the entry energy of $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{g}^{2+}$ $(463.1\mathrm{kJmol^{-1}})$ is much higher than $\mathrm{Li^{+}}$ $(135.1\mathrm{kJmol^{-1}},$ , especially the difference between $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{g}^{2+}$ and ${\bf L}\mathrm{i}^{+}$ is significantly increased. Similar findings can also be seen in $\operatorname{Li}[\mathbf{BCE}]^{+}@\mathbf{Z}\operatorname{IF}-7$ and Mg$[\mathrm{BCE}]^{2+}(\underline{{{a}}}\mathrm{ZIF}{-}7$ channels. The above phenomenon demonstrates that the electrostatic interaction between channel and cations changes from attraction to repulsion during the charge reversion from negative to positive, which increases the entry energy of cations. It should be noted that this process simultaneously expands the difference of entry energy (ΔE) between $\mathrm{Li^{+}}$ and $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{g}^{2+}$ (Figure 4a). The relationship between ΔE and selectivity was established (Figure S12), where the value of entry energy determines the magnitude of the ion flux, and the $\Delta\mathrm{E}$ determines the selectivity. $\Delta\mathrm{E}$ and selectivity roughly follow a linear law, and the larger the $\Delta\mathrm{E}$ , the higher the selectivity. We realize this relationship may provide an idea for designing more novel separation materials, based on a wider range of channel-ion interactions. In addition, the entry energy of ${\bf L i}^{+}$ varies less than that of $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{g}^{2+}$ in different channels, which explains the corresponding change of current values in Figure 3d. That is, the exclusion from a positively charged channel is more rigorous for a divalent cation compared to a monovalent cation.
## Conclusion
Our work demonstrates that the encapsulation ions within MOF membrane is an effective way to construct the charged MOF membrane with ideal selective combination. With the help of guest crown ethers, the MOF channel with effective charge inversion was successfully synthesized by crowning various valent cations. Experiments showed that the positively charged channel obtained a better $\mathrm{Li^{+}/M g^{2+}}$ selectivity, which originated from the expansion of difference of entry energy between $\mathrm{Li^{+}}$ and $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{g}^{2+}$ at subnanoscale, and it was not a simple electrostatic effect. In addition, the synthesis strategy adopted in this work will provide new insights to the preparation of tunable charging ion channels, and the findings provide experience for the design of high-efficiency ion separation membrane, especially for lithium extraction in the future.
